%I A399018 #15 Aug 27 2026 19:27:47
%S A399018 6,42,72,204,210,486,420,888,702,1410,1056,2052,1482,2814,1980,3696,
%T A399018 2550,4698,3192,5820,3906,7062,4692,8424,5550,9906,6480,11508,7482,
%U A399018 13230,8556,15072,9702,17034,10920,19116,12210,21318,13572,23640,15006,26082,16512,28644
%N A399018 Number of solid unit triangles in the hexagonal cross-section of the first iteration of the generalized Menger sponge with subdivision 2n+1, cut through the center perpendicular to a space diagonal.
%C A399018 The sponge: subdivide a cube into (2n+1)^3 equal cells, number the coordinates 0..2n along each axis, and keep a cell when at most one of its three coordinates is odd. n = 1 gives the first iteration of the classical Menger sponge, with A103532(1) = 20 cells kept.
%C A399018 The cut: the plane through the center perpendicular to a space diagonal meets the cube in a regular hexagon of side 2n+1. That hexagon is tiled by 6*(2n+1)^2 unit triangles of a triangular lattice; a(n) counts the solid ones and A399019(n) the empty ones, so a(n) + A399019(n) = 6*(2n+1)^2.
%C A399018 Cell by cell, writing c = 3n for the coordinate sum of the central cell: a kept cell with x+y+z = c meets the plane in a full hexagon of 6 unit triangles, a kept cell with x+y+z = c-1 or c+1 meets it in a single triangle, and every other cell misses the plane.
%C A399018 a(n) is always divisible by 6. The cross-section has 6-fold rotational symmetry about its center, and that center is a vertex of the triangular lattice rather than the centroid of a triangle, so no unit triangle is fixed by the rotation and the triangles fall into orbits of size 6. Hence a(n)/6 = 1, 7, 12, 34, 35, 81, 70, 148, 117, 235, ... is the number of solid triangles in one 60-degree sextant.
%C A399018 The central cell (n, n, n) is kept when n is even and removed when n is odd, so the unit hexagon at the center of the cross-section alternates solid, empty, solid, ...; this parity is the source of the period-2 split in the formulas below.
%C A399018 The even- and odd-indexed terms follow different quadratics, so a(n) is not monotonic: a(5) = 486 exceeds a(6) = 420, and a(7) = 888 exceeds a(8) = 702.
%H A399018 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="/A399018/b399018.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A399018 Paul Bourke, <a href="https://paulbourke.net/fractals/mrlymath/">mrly fractals: Menger, Sierpinski, Cantor</a>.
%H A399018 George Hart, <a href="https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2012/12/10/mathematical-impressions-the-surprising-menger-sponge-slice/">Mathematical Impressions: The Surprising Menger Sponge Slice</a>, Simons Foundation, 2012.
%H A399018 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="https://paulbourke.net/fractals/mrlymath/mrlymath.pdf">Marley Math: Cantor Sets, Sierpinski Carpets, Menger Sponges, And More</a>.
%H A399018 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="https://github.com/carlomitchener/carlomitchener/blob/main/some26/README.md">Generalized Menger Sponge Slice</a>, 2026.
%H A399018 Carlo Mitchener, <a href="/A399018/a399018.png">Illustration of a(1), a(2), a(3): the solid triangles, in black</a>.
%H A399018 <a href="/index/Rec#order_06">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (0,3,0,-3,0,1).
%F A399018 a(n) = 3*(n+1)*(3*n+2) for n even; a(n) = 3*(n+1)*(5*n+2) for n odd.
%F A399018 a(n) = 3*(n+1)*(4*n + 2 - n*(-1)^n).
%F A399018 a(n) = 3*a(n-2) - 3*a(n-4) + a(n-6) for n > 5.
%F A399018 G.f.: 6*(1 + x + 2*x^2)*(1 + 6*x + x^2)/((1-x)^3*(1+x)^3).
%F A399018 a(n) = 6*(2*n+1)^2 - A399019(n).
%F A399018 a(2*n) = 6*A033570(n); a(2*n+1) = 6*A195018(n+1).
%F A399018 a(n) - A399019(n) = 6*(2*n+1) - 6*n*(n+1)*(-1)^n; divided by 6 this is A028387(n) for odd n and -A028387(n-2) for even n >= 2.
%F A399018 a(2*n+1) = A399019(2*n) + 42*(2*n+1); a(2*n) = A399019(2*n-1) + 60*n for n >= 1.
%F A399018 E.g.f.: 3*((2 + 12*x + 3*x^2)*cosh(x) + (2 + 8*x + 5*x^2)*sinh(x)). - _Stefano Spezia_, Aug 16 2026
%e A399018 a(0) = 6: no cell is removed, so the cross-section is a plain hexagon of 6 unit triangles, all solid.
%e A399018 a(1) = 42: the cross-section of the first Menger sponge iteration holds 6*3^2 = 54 unit triangles, of which 42 are solid. The other 12 form a single six-pointed star, the largest of the star-shaped holes counted by A299916.
%t A399018 A399018[n_] := 3*(n + 1)*(2 - n*((-1)^n - 4)); Array[A399018, 50, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Aug 27 2026 *)
%o A399018 (Python)
%o A399018 def a(n): return 3*(n + 1)*(4*n + 2 - n*(-1)**n)
%o A399018 (Python)
%o A399018 from itertools import product
%o A399018 def a(n):
%o A399018     m = 2*n + 1
%o A399018     c = 3*(m - 1)//2
%o A399018     w = {c - 1: 1, c: 6, c + 1: 1}
%o A399018     return sum(w[s] for x, y, z in product(range(m), repeat=3) if (s := x + y + z) in w and (x%2) + (y%2) + (z%2) <= 1)
%Y A399018 Cf. A399019 (the empty triangles of the same cross-section).
%Y A399018 Cf. A299916 (the same count across iterations of the classical Menger sponge).
%Y A399018 Cf. A103532, A395241 (cells kept and cells removed by the same sponge).
%Y A399018 Cf. A033570, A195018 (the bisections of a(n)/6), A028387.
%K A399018 nonn,easy,new
%O A399018 0,1
%A A399018 _Carlo Mitchener_, Aug 16 2026
